<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saint-Nicolas-de-Port</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Saint-Nicolas-de-Port is a town on the Meurthe near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nancy">Nancy</a> in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. It grew around a relic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-nicholas">Saint Nicholas</a> brought from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bari">Bari</a> in the late eleventh century and became the chief pilgrimage centre and fair town of the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a>. Its large Flamboyant Gothic basilica, begun in 1481 after René II's victory over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bold">Charles the Bold</a>, is one of the finest churches of eastern France.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saint-nicolas-de-port.jpg" alt="Saint-Nicolas-de-Port" width="320" height="481" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Berthold Werner · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saint_Nicolas_de_Port_BW_1.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
